o adapters export `buildRolloutLines`/`buildSessionLines` precisely so `fabricate-invariants.test.ts` can exercise them. This is the root cause of the whole list: nothing forced the payload to be inspected. `scripts/smoke-interactive.mjs:189` also still hardcodes `[\"claude-code\", \"codex\"]`, and the README wasn't updated.\n- **No ledger capture path.** `conversation-ledger` has no opencode adapter (its README lists it as SQLite/plugin-API, unimplemented), so this is a one-way bridge: turns made in opencode are never captured, and the `fabricatedConversationId` lineage points at a conversation that will never reappear. Not necessarily wrong as a first step, but the PR doesn't say so, and the roadmap explicitly says \"deepen the two existing targets before broadening.\"\n\n## What it got right\n\nCommand surface (`-s <id>`, `import <file>`), the export/import envelope shape, the `Imported session: <id>` stdout regex (exact match, and re-import is idempotent), the picker-title convention, text/reasoning part shapes, and the tool-call-as-history framing. That's genuinely non-trivial reverse-engineering for a one-shot. The failure mode is characteristic: it inferred the format from the happy path and never validated the branch it couldn't see.\n\nSanity check on my own claims: build and all 77 existing tests pass on the branch (none touch opencode). I cleaned up after myself — deleted the three fabricated smoke sessions plus the orphan the failed import left behind, and removed `~/.turnbridge/imports/`; your original opencode session is untouched. I made no code changes and posted nothing to GitHub.\n\nresult: PR #3 reviewed and smoke-tested against opencode 1.18.5 — fabrication is broken for any conversation with a tool call (`state` requires `title`/`metadata`/`time`), failures leave partial sessions with no bootstrap fallback, and the shim hijacks `opencode -s <id>` into an infinite loop; 9 further correctness/convention issues listed, format reverse-engineering ot